**Proof.**

If $p=2$, then $1\le r<p$ forces $r=1$, and $r\mid k$ is trivial. So assume $p$ is odd.

Since $p$ is odd we may factor
$$p^{p}+1=(p+1)\Phi,\qquad \Phi=p^{p-1}-p^{p-2}+\cdots-p+1=\frac{p^{p}+1}{p+1}.$$

**Claim 1.** $\gcd(p+1,\Phi)=1$.

Evaluating $\Phi$ at $p\equiv -1\pmod{p+1}$, every term $(-1)^i p^{p-1-i}$ becomes $(-1)^i(-1)^{p-1-i}=(-1)^{p-1}=1$ (as $p-1$ is even), and there are $p$ terms, so $\Phi\equiv p\equiv -1\pmod{p+1}$. Hence $\gcd(\Phi,p+1)\mid\gcd(-1,p+1)=1$.

**Claim 2.** Every prime $q\mid\Phi$ satisfies $q\equiv 1\pmod{2p}$ (in particular $q\equiv 1\pmod p$).

Since $q\mid\Phi\mid p^{p}+1$, we have $p^{p}\equiv -1\pmod q$, so $p^{2p}\equiv1\pmod q$. The order $d$ of $p$ modulo $q$ divides $2p$ but not $p$ (as $p^{p}\equiv -1\neq1$), so $d\in\{2,2p\}$. If $d=2$, then $p\equiv -1\pmod q$, i.e. $q\mid p+1$; but $q\mid\Phi$ and $\gcd(\Phi,p+1)=1$, contradiction. (The case $p\equiv1\pmod q$ gives $p^{p}\equiv1\neq-1$.) Also $q=2$ cannot divide $\Phi$: by LTE, $v_2(p^{p}+1)=v_2(p+1)$ for odd $p$, so $v_2(\Phi)=0$. Thus $d=2p$, whence $2p\mid q-1$, i.e. $q\equiv1\pmod{2p}$.

Consequently any product of prime powers dividing $\Phi$ is $\equiv1\pmod p$.

Now write $N=pk+r$. Since $N\mid p^{p}+1=(p+1)\Phi$ with $\gcd(p+1,\Phi)=1$, we may uniquely write
$$N=D\cdot E,\qquad D\mid p+1,\quad E\mid\Phi,\quad \gcd(D,E)=1.$$

By Claim 2, $E\equiv1\pmod p$. Since $N\equiv r\pmod p$ (as $0<r<p$), we get
$$D\equiv N\cdot E^{-1}\equiv r\pmod p.$$

But $D\mid p+1$, so $1\le D\le p+1$. Combined with $D\equiv r\pmod p$ and $1\le r\le p-1$:

- If $r\ge2$: the only value in $[1,p+1]$ congruent to $r\pmod p$ is $D=r$ (since $r+p>p+1$). Thus $D=r$, so $r\mid p+1$, and $r\mid N=DE$. Hence $r\mid pk$; since $\gcd(r,p)=1$ (as $r<p$ with $p$ prime), we conclude $r\mid k$.
- If $r=1$: then $r\mid k$ is immediate.

In every case $r\mid k$, as required. $\blacksquare$

